{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.207,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "PROBABILITY"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.862,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "205"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.138,
            0.902,
            0.177
          ],
          "content": "Let Y be the event ‘the ball taken out is yellow’, B be the event ‘the ball taken out is blue’, and R be the event ‘the ball taken out is red’."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.184,
            0.515,
            0.203
          ],
          "content": "Now, the number of possible outcomes = 3."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.208,
            0.665,
            0.227
          ],
          "content": "(i) The number of outcomes favourable to the event $\\mathrm { Y } = 1$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.248,
            0.135,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.235,
            0.361,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{Y}) = \\frac{1}{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.283,
            0.564,
            0.324
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, (ii) $\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{R})=\\frac{1}{3} \\text { and }(\\mathrm{iii}) \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{B})=\\frac{1}{3}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.327,
            0.218,
            0.345
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Remarks :"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.353,
            0.902,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": "1. An event having only one outcome of the experiment is called an elementary event. In Example 1, both the events E and F are elementary events. Similarly, in Example 2, all the three events, Y, B and R are elementary events."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.416,
            0.562,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": "2. In Example 1, we note that : $\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{E}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{F}) = 1$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.442,
            0.608,
            0.461
          ],
          "content": "In Example 2, we note that : $\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{Y}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{R}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{B}) = 1$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.467,
            0.902,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": "Observe that **the sum of the probabilities of all the elementary events of an experiment** is 1. This is true in general also."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.518,
            0.902,
            0.577
          ],
          "content": "**Example 3 :** Suppose we throw a die once. (i) What is the probability of getting a number greater than 4 ? (ii) What is the probability of getting a number less than or equal to 4 ?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.584,
            0.902,
            0.642
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** (i) Here, let E be the event ‘getting a number greater than $4 ^ { \\circ }$ . The number of possible outcomes is six : 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6, and the outcomes favourable to E are 5 and 6. Therefore, the number of outcomes favourable to E is 2. So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.315,
            0.65,
            0.735,
            0.693
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{E}) = \\mathrm{P}(\\text { number greater than } 4) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.697,
            0.712,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Let F be the event ‘getting a number less than or equal to $4 ^ { \\circ }$ ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.724,
            0.426,
            0.743
          ],
          "content": "Number of possible outcomes = 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.75,
            0.575,
            0.769
          ],
          "content": "Outcomes favourable to the event F are 1, 2, 3, 4."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.777,
            0.565,
            0.796
          ],
          "content": "$\\mathrm { S o } ,$ , the number of outcomes favourable to F is 4."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.816,
            0.205,
            0.836
          ],
          "content": "Therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.316,
            0.804,
            0.472,
            0.845
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{F})=\\frac{4}{6}=\\frac{2}{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.967,
            0.564,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
